> 
> I don't think just teleporting down to the Valley should be worth more
> than surviving the Quest. It were better to skip it completely and use
> just the count of invocation items.

OK, I've been doing some stats on this:

completed Quest but no Gehennom: 2 people, 3 games
made Gehennom w/out Quest: 2 people, 3 games

So it's not like we're dealing with a major distinction here.  ;-)

got bell, but no book or candelabrum: 4 people, 5 games
got bell and candlebrum, but no book: 1 person, 1 game
any other combination: none

So, again, not a lot o' variety in this set either.

To me, this argues that it's better to use the "got the bell" flag in the logs
to indicate Quest completion rather than to do an (admitedly nifty) three-part
trophy for the invocation items since it stays linear with the game plot and
the log data does not provide strong support for leaving the game sequence with
the star trophies.

The game doesn't make you achieve things in any particular order (with a few
significant exceptions), but it certainly does have a plot sequence if only in
the order in which it presents challenges to the player.  Anyone who manages to
dive to the Valley before they've finished the Quest is bypassing a major game
plot element to do it, and they're taking quite a risk as well.

Sorry, Philipp; I can see your point, but I think we're going to go with Iron
being the Quest and Copper being Gehennom.

